{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Before Ba'al Zephon.</b> Rashi explained: it alone remained of all the gods of Egypt, in order to mislead them. Pharaoh thought in his heart: it is misleading them, so that they may agree to my decree to destroy them in the water — I will go and worship it. Therefore it is said: ופרעה הקריב — 'Pharaoh brought near' is not said, but rather הקריב — this teaches that he brought an offering before it. Another interpretation: he brought Israel near to do repentance." "<b>Opposite it you shall encamp.</b> The Rav, Rabbi Judah, wondered: how could He tell them to encamp by the sea before Ba'al Zephon, when we say that it is forbidden for a person to say to his fellow, 'Wait for me by such-and-such idol'? This requires investigation. And it seems to me that this applies specifically to a person, but not to the Holy One, blessed be He — for we find that the Holy One, blessed be He, sits and judges the entire world even on the Sabbath, and even on Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim, even though for Israel it is forbidden. (And Rashi z\"l wrote similarly in [ר\"ה] (fol. 30a) s.v. 'alternatively, stones for nights etc.' — that is, by a building of human construction etc. — see there.) And furthermore, it seems to me that the Torah had not yet been given, so there is no concern, even though it was already written and laid before the Holy One, blessed be He."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:09.319827848Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}